<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of the Pyrenees</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7 November <a href="/years/1659">1659</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/november-7">On this day: 7 November</a> · Also known as Peace of the Pyrenees · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-spanish-war-1635-1659">Franco-Spanish War (1635–1659)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> was signed on 7 November 1659 on Pheasant Island in the Bidasoa river, ending the Franco-Spanish War that had begun in 1635. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a>, Artois and a string of towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, Hainaut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> married the Infanta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>. It marked France's replacement of Spain as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s leading power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees.jpg" alt="Treaty of the Pyrenees" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Laumosnier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traite-Pyrenees.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> after the Spanish Habsburg victories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> in the 1620s and at the Battle of Nördlingen in 1634. By 1640 France was aiding the revolt in Catalonia, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> responded in 1648 by aiding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fronde">Fronde</a> in France. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 France gained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundgau">Sundgau</a> and cut Spanish access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, which increased hostilities. An Anglo-French alliance won the Battle of the Dunes on 14 June 1658, but the war ground to a halt the next year when the French campaign to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> was defeated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was negotiated and signed on Pheasant Island, in the Bidasoa River on the border between the two countries, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a> and their chief ministers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> and Don Luis Méndez de Haro. France gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpignan">Perpignan</a>, and the northern half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a>, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmedy">Montmédy</a> and other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, Artois and towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, Béthune, Gravelines and Thionville. A new border was fixed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. Because the treaty spoke only of villages, the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llivia">Llívia</a> was unintentionally left to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as an exclave, and the border was not properly settled until the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> of 1856.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was forced to recognise the French gains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>. In exchange, the French king pledged to stop supporting Portugal and renounced his claim to the Principality of Catalonia. The treaty also arranged the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>, daughter of Philip IV, who renounced her claim to the Spanish throne in return for a dowry that was never paid, a factor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a> of 1667. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> and sold it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1662.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The historian J. H. Elliott sees the treaty as the decisive admission of defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s long struggle to maintain the Pax Hispanica. Paul Kennedy identifies it as the climax of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> bid for mastery, and attributes Spain's fall to imperial overstretch. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a>'s last major diplomatic achievement, and together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> stability and diplomatic advantage. By 1660 most of the European continent was at peace, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> had ended the dominance of the Habsburgs. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> the treaty established border customs and restricted the free flow of people and goods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of the Pyrenees” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Louis XIV's wars then dismembered it: the <b>Treaty of the Pyrenees</b> in 1659 and the Treaty of Nijmegen in 1678 gave France Valenciennes, Maubeuge, Condé, Le Quesnoy and Avesnes, which became French Hainaut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of the Pyrenees</b> in 1659 ceded Roussillon and Artois to France, and in 1668 Spain accepted Portuguese independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Barcelona surrendered to Philip IV’s forces in 1652, and by the <b>Treaty of the Pyrenees</b> in 1659 Roussillon and northern Cerdanya were ceded to France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Westphalia in 1648, in which the Treaty of Münster recognised Dutch independence after eighty years of the most expensive war in Spanish imperial history, confirmed Spain's loss of hegemony in northern Europe, and the <b>Peace of the Pyrenees</b> (1659) acknowledged the ascendancy of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XIII took Arras in 1640, and the <b>Treaty of the Pyrenees</b> of 1659 gave most of the county to the Kingdom of France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-artois">County of Artois</a></li><li><blockquote>It became part of the Crown of Aragon, and in 1659 the <b>Treaty of the Pyrenees</b> split the valley between Spain and France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-cerdanya">County of Cerdanya</a></li></ul></section>
